The absence of petechiae or other “typical” signs of smothering weakens the prosecution theory somewhat, but it does not rule it out. Smothering can be very difficult to prove pathologically, and its classic signs are not always present. So this point reduces the strength of the murder-by-smothering explanation, but only moderately.

Putting the evidence together:

- Factors suggesting guilt:
  - two infant deaths in the same family, both apparently occurring while Jane was present;
  - Ben’s autopsy reportedly found rib and spinal injuries, hypoxic brain damage, and eye/eyelid haemorrhages, which can be viewed as suspicious for inflicted trauma/asphyxial events;
  - retrospective review of Andy’s case also found bruising, torn frenulum, and blood in the lung.

- Factors suggesting caution / innocence:
  - Andy’s original post-mortem suggested natural causes;
  - SIDS is a real alternative explanation, even if rare twice in one family;
  - the injuries on Ben were not noted by nurses when he arrived;
  - retrospective reinterpretation of earlier evidence can be less dependable than contemporaneous findings;
  - absence of petechiae and other classic signs makes the specific smothering theory less secure.

Overall, this still looks suspicious, but the evidential picture remains medically and inferentially uncertain. The added fact about absence of typical smothering signs lowers my earlier assessment somewhat.

40

Taking the evidence as a whole, the case is mixed but now leans toward guilt more than innocence.

Main points supporting guilt:
- two infant deaths in the same family under strikingly similar circumstances;
- Jane was alone with both babies when they were found collapsed;
- Ben had hypoxic brain damage and eye/eyelid haemorrhages consistent with smothering or violent trauma;
- the doctor alleged the hypoxic damage may have begun hours before death;
- on re-examination, Andy’s bruises, torn frenulum, and blood in the lung are said to be indicative of abuse and characteristic of smothering.

Main points supporting doubt:
- Andy’s original post-mortem suggested natural causes;
- some previously suspicious findings have been weakened:
  - spinal bleeding may be common in natural death,
  - rib findings may be due to resuscitation or post-mortem change,
  - no petechiae or classic definitive signs of smothering were found;
- retrospective reinterpretation of pathology can be less reliable than contemporaneous findings;
- several medical findings are described as “consistent with” rather than uniquely diagnostic.

Overall, the repeated pattern plus the accepted abusive interpretation of Andy’s later-reviewed injuries pushes the case above even odds. But the medical ambiguities still leave substantial room for doubt, so I would not put it extremely high.

